Abstract
To explore the influence of different factors on the atomization pattern and characteristics of the crossflow, the influence of three dimensionless parameters is investigated in this paper, namely Weber number, Oh, and the momentum flux ratio q, on the atomization in subsonic crossflow. The investigations are based on the CLSVOF method and Large Eddy Simulation and developed in conjunction with adaptive mesh refinement techniques. In terms of atomization morphology, three-view drawing of the jet fragmentation morphology and cross-sectional deformation processes under different operating conditions are compared and investigated. In terms of atomization characteristics, the jet penetration depth and spraying distribution curves, as well as the fragmentation position and droplet velocity distribution are analyzed in detail. The results show that there is a pronounced effect for Weber number on the fragmentation pattern and atomization characteristics of liquid jet into a crossflow. As the Weber number increases, the column fragmentation dominated by RT instability gradually changes to surface fragmentation dominated by KH instability. The research also concluded that the momentum flux ratio q is a key element in the penetration depth, which is reflected in the fact that when q is larger, the jet bends less with a stronger penetration ability.
Introduction
In recent years, the great impact of the increase of carbon emissions makes the issue of carbon neutral continue to attract attention all over the world. As an important component and support of global economic activities, the carbon emission of air transportation industry represents about 10% of the transportation industry, and the carbon emission from aviation fuel combustion accounts for about 79% of its total. In order to improve the fuel efficiency of aerospace engines and reduce pollutant emissions, new combustion chamber design and advanced combustion technology have become research hotspots. The atomization process of liquid fuel in the combustion chamber will be affected by complex aerodynamic conditions such as strong turbulence disturbance, unsteady inflow jet and strong pressure. The processes of droplet breakup, flow, evaporation and mixing show highly unsteady and complex gas-liquid two-phase interface structure. The atomization of liquid fuel has become one of the important problems in the combustion efficiency and stability of the engine. Liquid jet in crossflow (LJIC) is a method of atomization in which liquid fuel is sprayed vertically into a subsonic or supersonic gas stream. The atomization is both simple and efficient and is widely used in the combustion chamber of gas turbine engines, subsonic and super combustion ram engines, etc. The atomization characteristics of LJIC depend on three main categories of variables. The first category is liquid-phase parameters, including jet velocity, liquid-phase density, liquid-phase surface tension, and liquid-phase viscosity. The second category is the gas-phase parameters, which are gas velocity, density, and viscosity. The third category is the geometric parameters, mainly for the diameter of the spray hole, injection angle, and the shape of the spray hole. However, the liquid jet vertical injection into the transverse airflow is adopted in most engines, and the round hole type is widely used.
According to the law of dimensionless analysis, the above three types of variables can be transformed into dimensionless parameters, which are shown in Eq. (1), (2), (3). They respectively are: (1) the gas Weber number We, which represents the ratio of aerodynamic force and liquid surface tension. (2) the Ohnesorge number Oh, which is the ratio of liquid viscous force and surface tension. (3) the momentum flux ratio q, which represents the ratio of liquid-phase momentum and gas-phase momentum. The gas Weber number is an important parameter to study different crushing morphology and mechanism. General results show that when Oh is less than 0.1, the crushing morphology is less influenced by Oh. While when Oh is greater than 0.1, Oh has a significant effect on the crushing morphology. q is the key parameter of jet penetration depth. As q takes a larger value, the jet bends less and penetrates deeper. Conversely, at a smaller value of q, the jet bends more and penetrates shallower.
