For a thorough investigation of the atomization mechanism of LJIC, a large number of experimental and simulation studies have been conducted. The research is mainly conducted in three aspects which are the atomization mechanism, penetration depth, and liquid mist dispersion [[1], [2], [3], [4]].
Nambu et al. [5] studied the primary atomization mechanism of liquid jet in crossflow, in which the effect of RT instability on the breakup and droplet distribution was analyzed in detail. It was concluded that RT instability mainly existed in the edge area of the jet column and played an important role in the fragmentation of the jet. And when RT instability was significant, the breakup of the jet was more likely to produce smaller liquid filaments, and the size distribution of droplets changed obviously. Mukundan et al. [6] simulated the primary atomization characteristics of liquid jet in air crossflow with the value of We at 330, using direct numerical simulation (DNS) and large eddy simulation (LES) methods. The analysis results of droplet diameter data obeyed a lognormal distribution. Column/bag breakup and ligement/surface breakup had been observed synchronously.
Yi [7] used numerical simulations to probe the perturbation changes on the surface of an incompressible viscous liquid jet. The break-up of low-velocity jets was considered to be the result of evolving axisymmetric perturbations caused by surface tension. In contrast, the break-up and atomization of high-velocity jets were due to aerodynamically induced non-axisymmetric perturbations. Xu [8,9] comparatively studied the non-constant transverse injection of kerosene in stationary air, subsonic airflow, and supersonic airflow. The results showed that the fragmentation and atomization characteristics of kerosene in subsonic gas streams were similar to those of supersonic gas streams. However, in the subsonic flow, the break-up position of the liquid column moved up the jet column and penetrated to a greater depth. It was also concluded that the physical parameters of the fuel had a weak influence on the atomization characteristics and the structure of the shock wave of the jet column in subsonic and supersonic airflows. Liu [10] used the CLSVOF method to perform direct simulations of the jet fragmentation process. It was concluded that the jet column in the transverse flow was deformed and formed the surface wave under the combined effect of RT instability and KH instability, which was accompanied by the continuous stripping of small droplets during the process. The droplet breakup at different Weber numbers and Ohnesorge numbers by the S-CLSVOF method with the adaptive mesh refinement (AMR) technique was investigated by Chu et al. [11]. Using AMR near the interface could greatly reduce the calculation time and storage cost compared with global mesh refinement. The results showed that when the aerodynamic force was dominant, the increase of droplet viscous force would prolong the breaking time of droplets, but it had little effect on the final kinetic energy of droplet.
The initial fragmentation of the liquid jet in the transverse flow varies in its fragmentation pattern over a range of Weber numbers [[12], [13], [14]]. Based on the jet atomization experimental platform, Lan [15] investigated the effect of different gas-liquid physical parameters on the initial fragmentation pattern and jet penetration trajectory characteristics under the action of low-velocity cross-flow. The study concluded that the gas-liquid momentum flux ratio and the liquid Weber number jointly determined the fragmentation mode of the jet under low-velocity crossflow conditions. Magallon [16] carried out an experimental study of the deformation and fragmentation of a liquid jet in a subsonic transverse gas flow at room temperature and normal pressure. The results showed that the surface fragmentation process of a liquid jet was similar to the secondary fragmentation of a liquid droplet, while the atomization states corresponding to different gas Weber number ranges were given. They concluded that the jet fragmentation characteristics were mainly influenced by the Weber number. Sallam [17] conducted experimental research on cylindrical non-turbulent jets in subsonic transverse flows utilizing pulse shadowgraph and hologram observations. The experiments indicated that the fragmentation behavior of jets in gas-phase transverse flows had many similarities to the secondary fragmentation of small droplets under surge disturbance. Wang [18] experimentally studied the columnar fragmentation process of a circular jet in the Weber number range of 1.7–7. The experimental observations revealed that as the logarithm of the Weber number of the gas increased, the surface wave wavelength decreased, whereas the diameter of the fragmented droplet increased. In addition, Wang [19] also carried out an experimental study on the bag fragmentation of liquid circular jets in a transverse airflow field. It was found that the fragmentation length of the bag was independent of the liquid jet velocity, but only related to and linearly related to the Weber number. The relationship between the core jet fragmentation position and the liquid-gas momentum flux ratio q was also derived.
The penetration depth and the trajectory of the jet are important parameters for describing the mixing characteristics of the transverse jet [20]. Wu [21,22] carried out an experimental study on the fragmentation process of transverse jets using the pulsed shadow imaging method. The fragmentation process of jets with different liquid jets, different nozzle diameters, and different incoming Mach numbers were investigated successively. The surface wave phenomenon, liquid column trajectory, and liquid column fragmentation position of the liquid column were analyzed, and it was concluded that the gas-liquid momentum flux ratio was an important factor affecting the jet trajectory. Jadidi [23] investigated the fragmentation of jets at low Weber numbers in transverse flows, showing that both the momentum flux ratio and the nozzle aspect ratio significantly altered the liquid penetration height. In the case of the given momentum flux ratio and the Weber number, columnar fragmentation occurred later, penetrated higher, and atomized better for round jets than that for the elliptical jet. Zhu [24] explored the mechanism of deformation and fragmentation of a liquid circular jet in a transverse airflow by using a high-speed camera filming method. Through processing and analysis of the high-frequency dynamic fragmentation process images, it was considered that the bending degree of the jet decreased as the liquid-air momentum flux ratio increased. A relationship between the coordinates of the average breaking point and the liquid to gas momentum flux ratio was also given. The influences of different diameters and types of nozzles on the atomization characteristics of liquid jet in subsonic crossflow were investigated by Broumand et al. [25]. The variation trend of jet structure, jet trajectory and breaking length under different outlet conditions was explored. The results showed that the jet surface was relatively smooth in the range of low injection velocity.
The mass distribution, size distribution, and velocity distribution of the liquid mist can be used to assess the mixing effect of the fuel and air [26]. Aalburg [27,28] had successively inquired into the deformation of liquid jets under aerodynamic action, the conditions of jet fragmentation, as well as the dimensions and velocities of liquid filaments and droplets on the surface of liquid columns. He believed that the effect of cross-flow conditions on the mathematical relationship between droplet SMD and flow distance was not significant. Li [29] mainly investigated the influence of the viscosity factor of the liquid jet on the details of the atomization process of the jet in the transverse airflow with the help of numerical simulations. The results of atomization of high-viscosity jets were compared with those of atomization of low-viscosity jets. The results concluded that, firstly, the jet fragmentation pattern shifted as the viscosity of the liquid increased and the size of the atomized droplets increased. Secondly, the droplet size distribution of the high viscosity jet was characterized by a shift towards a single-peaked distribution, which was very different from the double-peaked distribution observed at low viscosity jet conditions. Mei [30]. used different VOF-DPM coupling methods to explore the paraffin jet fragmentation morphology and atomization trajectory under the conditions of Weber number We = 167.9 and the momentum flux ratio q = 16. The SMD spatial distribution law of paraffin droplets was also summarized. It was concluded that the effect of the gas Weber number atomization pattern cannot be neglected. Du et al. [31] numerically studied the fuel penetration depth, mixing efficiency and flow field properties of steady-state jet and different pulsed jet (sine wave, square wave and triangle wave, etc.) under the condition of supersonic crossflow. It was found that compared with the steady-state jet, the pulsed jet with different wave shapes was conducive to improve the mixing efficiency of the jet.
In summary, although the initial fragmentation process of liquid jets under air crossflow conditions has been investigated extensively by many scholars over the years, the relevant conclusions are not comprehensive enough. The effect of the dimensionless parameters for the jet in crossflow on the jet morphology and atomization characteristics remains to be further investigated. Therefore, this paper addresses this issue with the aim of deepening the understanding of atomization regulations and laying the foundation for the establishment of a high-precision initial atomization model. In this paper, the Large Eddy Simulation (LES) is used as the turbulence model, the CLSVOF method as the interface tracking method with adaptive mesh refinement technique, to investigate the atomization of liquid jets in subsonic crossflow. The influences of the dimensionless parameters We, Oh and q on jet atomization morphology and atomization characteristics (including penetration depth, spreading boundary line, jet column break-up point location, and droplet velocity) are mainly investigated.
